A||||4||batboy||computer hotrodding revisited||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||05-09-2000||11:59 PM||||My custom built computer has been chugging along just fine for 15 months now. When I first put it together, it was a killer system. Now it's seems tired and slow, even though I'm overclocking my Celeron 300A CPU to 450 MHz. I have an Abit BH6 motherboard that is still in great condition, so I upgraded the flash BIOS on my board and decided to do a low budget upgrade.<P>After a little research, I found out the new Intel Celeron 566 CPUs are the best bargain going right now if you have a BX chipset motherboard. These Celeron 566 chips are the same "Coppermine" cores that the newest line of Pentium III chips use. The only exception is that the Celeron has only 128k cache compared to the P-III's 256k.<P>These Celeron 566 chips are "supposed" to use a 66 MHz bus speed, but the P-III brothers are being ran at 100 and even 133 MHz. Most of the Celeron 566 CPU's will overclock at 100 MHz bus speed which bumps the processor clock speed up to a whopping 850 MHz!!!<P>For $230 (US) I found that PowerUp Corp. out of Texas sells Celeron 566 chips guaranteed to overclock to 850 and includes the converter card you need to adapt the chip to your motherboard and a Golden Orb cooler/fan.<P>Compare this to a $800 retail box P-III 850 chip and you tell me which one is the best bargain. I'm going to try it, probably order it next week, I'll keep you posted.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shiner||05-10-2000||02:10 AM||||Check around, and you can get a P3 600mhz chip with a 133mhz bus speed for a bit over $200, and that is retail, which includes a fan and warranty (I belive 3 years).  Slap a 128mb pc133 ram on for around $100, and get a mobo that will support all this stuff.  You would have a flying system.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||05-10-2000||10:04 AM||||Yeah, I thought about getting a P-3 600E 100MHz (Coppermine) chip, but they're $300-$350, plus another $125 for the mobo and $100-$125 for memory, overclocking it to 133MHz bus speed for about 800MHz clock speed, but that's three times more money. It is true that a 800 P-3 will equal if not out perform an overclocked Celeron running at 850, but I'm looking strongly at low budget upgrade this time around.<P>I already have someone interested in my old Celeron 300A and cooler since I can guarantee  it will overclock to 450 (been doing so for over a year at default voltage). This means my actually cash out of pocket for the Celeron 566 overclocked to 850 route is only about $150, not a bad way to nearly double your "horsepower" if you have a tweakable BX motherboard like the Abit BH6. Gotta save money for my Fieros...<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||frontal lobe||05-10-2000||05:21 PM||||Celeron 566's on pricewatch go for about $160 and $10 shipping.  But they aren't guaranteed to be overclockers and it doesn't include the slot adapter and cooling fan.<P>So are you planning to keep your same motherboard?  You already are using pc100 ram since you are overclocking, right?<P>That's a pretty mighty performance boost for not a lot of money since your mobo and ram are perfectly fine.<P>Question, did overclocking affect your graphics card?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-10-2000||08:01 PM||||Yes, I'm using my old motherboard (Abit BH6), I've just flashed the BIOS with the newest version so it'll handle the newer chips now.<P>Yeah, I figure if I bought all the components separately for the best price, I'd save $20-$30, but no guarantee that it will go 850 MHz. I've heard that some mail-order vendors are testing and picking out the best chips for overclocking and selling the marginal ones as unknowns. I'll gladly spend an extra $20 or so for a guaranteed 850 MHz of zooming speed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My RAM is a 128 meg stick of Memory Man's PC100 CAS 2 which is supposed to be rated to 112-124 bus speeds (regular PC100 is CAS 3). I'll be using the same 100 MHz bus speed on the Celeron 566 as I did on my Celery 300A anyway, so no need for change there. The RAM has been working fine for 15 months now.<P>I have a Diamond Viper 550 3D video card in the AGP slot, it runs perfect. That's because the Abit boards automatically adjusts the PCI and AGP bus speeds back to default (33 & 66 MHz) when you are running 100 MHz front side bus speed (remember: P-2 and some P-3 chips use a 100 MHz FSB). That's the beauty of this Celeron overclocking setup I have, just basically a chip change to nearly double my fun.||||reg||1
